#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120877} :activity/id #uuid "ea872253-9665-5737-9ff4-e1c4c5546bda" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050426 :topic/id #uuid "47f4b767-dd8b-5a07-a614-8ff85185d0eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1861} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121122 :tutorial/content "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = 0,$ then we say that the two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly dependent**.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider two sets of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/46/46c22d599752ded730a67bfd57b4dfe238e512f6dff2d561afaf2f145c8118c2.svg)\n\n- For the first set ${\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is true only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ is *linearly independent*.\n\n- On the other hand, for the second set ${\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has solutions other than $x_1=x_2=0.$ For example, $x_1=2$ and $x_2=1$ gives\n![](images/14/145f500e491087587478f03bd52ae180a7b4237acf17db0317ca07d28f389c56.svg)\nTherefore, the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ is *not* linearly independent. Rather, it is *linearly dependent*.\n\n**Note:** You may notice that the vectors in the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ were parallel (i.e. multiples of one another), while the vectors in set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ were not. This trend holds in general, for all sets of two vectors:\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel, while\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly independent* if and only if the two vectors are not parallel." :tutorial/id #uuid "938aba59-31f8-5ad9-8d3d-ce9287feb3de" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6811 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8b84b116-3328-5055-9d5b-02360cff09e6" :step/math-academy-id 41352 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120878}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121123 :tutorial/content "Any set of two vectors that contains the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ must be linearly dependent. To see this, consider the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.$ We can realize that this set is linearly dependent using either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} + 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v} + x_2 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a solution $x_1=0$ and $x_2=1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set is linearly dependent.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, recall that a set of two vectors is linearly dependent if and only if the two vectors are parallel. That is, the two vectors are multiples of each other.\n\nThe zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to every other vector because it is a multiple of any other vector. For any vector $\\mathbf{v},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{0} = 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\mathbf{0} \\parallel \\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nSo, because $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{v},$ the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "ef2c96dd-d1cd-593e-a998-ba1be6c42686" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6812 :tutorial/title "Sets of Two Vectors Containing the Zero Vector"} :step/id #uuid "e814b4bf-688c-57df-8849-f4f2455723dd" :step/math-academy-id 41353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120879}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119497 :question/id #uuid "cbabcd64-6a33-552d-9f2f-704a9df44d1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10906"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048315}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564529}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"} :step/id #uuid "efae78f1-ab53-50ee-aa00-035536697ae6" :step/math-academy-id 41354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120880}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120880 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121124 :tutorial/content "So far, we've discussed linear dependence and linear independence for sets of two vectors. However, these concepts can be extended to sets of any number of vectors.\n\nA set of $n$ vectors $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\right\\}$ is called **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + x_n \\mathbf{v}_n = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1=x_2=\\ldots=x_n=0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = \\ldots = x_n = 0,$ then we say that the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\right\\}$ is **linearly dependent**.\n\nSimilar to sets containing two vectors, a general set of $n$ vectors is dependent if *any* two vectors in the set are parallel.\n\n**But be careful!** When working with sets containing more than two vectors, having a linearly dependent set is possible even if *none* of the vectors are parallel! \n\nFor example, consider the following set of vectors: \n\n![](images/bd/bd4df860628c44295dfd621e6b0e64839c2657c2baa094def656ea8b90ece9c8.svg)\n\nNone of the vectors in this set are parallel. However, we have\n\n![](images/50/5048e293fd6bb60a0ca95451aebd3bb77d0f748bb54e9f5dd044f60fb34c28ce.svg)\n\nSo, the equation\n\n![](images/8a/8ab4013ae0d9241268d040220efcd6184e23548e0685802cd6825b59a7bc71b1.svg)\n\nhas a solution $x_1 = 1,$ $x_2 = 1,$ and $x_3 = -1,$ which is different from $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that the vectors are linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "0d550a61-4219-5ffc-83a2-155ddd8fa9f4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6813 :tutorial/title "Linear Dependence and Independence for a General Set of Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "46556bbf-0804-5a49-af3c-3a10e60ee378" :step/math-academy-id 41357 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120881}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121125 :tutorial/content "As with sets containing only two vectors, any set of vectors that contains the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ must be linearly dependent. \n\nTo see this, consider the set \n\n$$\n\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_1 + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} + 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + x_{n-1} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} + x_n \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution $x_1=x_2=\\ldots = x_{n-1}=0$ and $x_n=1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=\\ldots = x_n=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "7270de59-1d83-5820-aa64-a5f4ca262e3e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6814 :tutorial/title "General Sets of Vectors Containing the Zero Vector"} :step/id #uuid "50295cf6-d08a-5be4-b71d-2116227de4f2" :step/math-academy-id 41358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120882}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119498 :question/id #uuid "7b28b42f-0ac4-543e-baeb-51534aaad480" :question/math-academy-id "e-10907"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572667}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"} :step/id #uuid "8247f0d4-142e-58a3-b2ed-d612accf2a6c" :step/math-academy-id 41359 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120883}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119499 :question/id #uuid "3843dc13-0908-5d4c-adfe-836a928a1410" :question/math-academy-id "e-10908"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564487}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"} :step/id #uuid "4922cdd4-be71-550d-b216-e5a30b232248" :step/math-academy-id 41362}] :activity/title "Linear Dependence and Independence" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120052}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572785} :activity/id #uuid "435f274d-0b23-584f-9037-052795dbc290" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050426 :topic/id #uuid "47f4b767-dd8b-5a07-a614-8ff85185d0eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1861} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275385 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2254be2b-f993-5682-a9c7-9d6c1aa8a420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449762} {:answer/id #uuid "b8837dad-34d4-5dd1-84f6-3b476d98748a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449763}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449763} :answer-field/id #uuid "00d1f4c0-204e-570d-a62f-76800bbc9dc9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450835} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f5e3379-8dc5-5670-a262-f7d4e0946e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449764} {:answer/id #uuid "91f14290-9d9f-5a69-bb74-76a89ebaae95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449765}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449764} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe4e04b9-477d-5ea2-9aec-04f3b5e68d21" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450836} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f1c79b1-32bb-523b-94bf-1ff6d3e1aa51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449766} {:answer/id #uuid "cea580c5-3155-5e7e-a21a-f98e021f5230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449766} :answer-field/id #uuid "35bfd7e7-1f66-5e4f-b278-bba2b1f1edc9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a541a49-3d67-515c-a6d4-f4d315e78809" :question/math-academy-id "q-157238" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3}=0.$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- **III.** The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-8+6 \\\\ -1+10-9 \\\\ 7+2-9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=2$ and ${x}_{3}=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "401746c1-ec37-5e0a-aaa3-3a448bcd11c8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572762 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae02702d-cbaa-5d1a-aa13-b56e62ddc9bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467292} {:answer/id #uuid "8beff570-6962-575d-83e8-fba6dd1988f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467293}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467293} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa0b3013-1ffe-5f8b-9931-4aa005775eb9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468367} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bf5efc0-d0b0-5ab3-9ccc-0f8c702a8c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467294} {:answer/id #uuid "16afad4c-9e4f-5af8-9660-d5618a99eb7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467295}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467294} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f0fc8a3-12a0-5de6-975a-2b93da0b8887" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468368} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c50196f4-ea00-5775-b667-c59254cbbc4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467296} {:answer/id #uuid "b5af3771-12a3-55ad-b5c8-8f960c1dfa83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467297}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467297} :answer-field/id #uuid "496267ed-e481-5779-8831-27ec9128e7bf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b1abddd4-93e9-501d-bf47-b8161bdb88cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-34714" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- **II.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- **III.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false."} :step/id #uuid "4da39b44-6aeb-5bbf-add4-ea308291967d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572786}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572667 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123e6b37-f5d0-58a4-a315-71252699ea45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572335} {:answer/id #uuid "3781813b-d8b2-5b24-921b-dce30178d1ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572381} {:answer/id #uuid "49b8843e-8d82-574e-a9c8-a640d411e4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572405} {:answer/id #uuid "7217c7fb-bda2-5564-844d-cd64e72c50f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572447} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f980f5-230b-530c-bbfe-2be36a54ca2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572550}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572405} :answer-field/id #uuid "24809e24-c6b5-5bd7-a31f-2f0864640659" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0bcf3acb-d9f4-5535-b8d1-de8e7ee2e9d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-157235" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $3{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3} & =3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6-4-2 \\\\ 9+5-14 \\\\ -3+3+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=3,\\,{x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{3}=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "746b862d-6a87-5a17-baae-0ed2efa306a0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572757}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572785 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319150 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dfbe41c-73e7-5f56-8ce0-fab54ad50616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467286} {:answer/id #uuid "f341d81e-bf26-5210-b2f0-54763ec31dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467287} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf382855-5600-5598-8164-5bd2d028dbce" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468364} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5748a03b-cb2b-5a33-8622-5e3f40a35b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467288} {:answer/id #uuid "114a823e-72e5-59c8-8d1c-742efeb3c793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467289}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467288} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8ad6860-dfb4-5757-801b-d9850857f91a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468365} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "861d0168-10b8-592b-ae21-e0ad86936c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467290} {:answer/id #uuid "8b18b4b9-7ea2-5329-bec7-e618d7c6db3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467291}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467291} :answer-field/id #uuid "e64595f5-23c8-52a5-9fe4-2c3e7d56935c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0c6e61ad-5f9a-5a1b-a420-c0ce7a3cc35c" :question/math-academy-id "q-34700" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n- **II.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- **III.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statement II is false, while statements I and III are true."} :step/id #uuid "a2e9ee89-f63b-5ff5-9ce0-9117e9d5dc82" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572762}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572786 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3917c546-1a71-5b69-95fa-031bd8a95f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468148} {:answer/id #uuid "685efbb1-a98a-5e24-81fb-c69c96152f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468149}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468149} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b4d8e60-f36f-5898-86ce-56958409039b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468795} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "799189fe-d9b2-592f-8d4d-7c1f35029fbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468150} {:answer/id #uuid "4b25fdd6-dd1d-55bd-b563-27dbc04b5ac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468150} :answer-field/id #uuid "24d36265-d1fc-567a-8fcc-21fe29ff2d57" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468796} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c73bf89d-de06-5c8f-ac60-6e163bb23230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468152} {:answer/id #uuid "0777f273-c934-5fa7-a08b-0aa490e67340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468153}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468152} :answer-field/id #uuid "22390857-93d9-508c-9a5a-4191c39a0625" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2f25df3d-4b0c-5b67-901d-8d878d2769ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-75655" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- **II.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- **III.** The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 12{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements II and III are false, while statement I is true."} :step/id #uuid "ab4db100-10bf-573b-91c6-3dab259c5b24" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572772}}] :activity/title "Linear Dependence and Independence" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572303}]}
